NAMESPACES

name:parent namespace:initial steward:default currency
e.g.
"gc":"chep.mon.uk":"jw.chep.mon.uk":"hrs.chep.mon.uk"


CURRENCIES

name:parent namespace:initial steward:prefix:suffix:default account name
e.g.
"hrs":"chep.mon.uk":"jw.chep.mon.uk":"":"h":"hrs"


ACCOUNTS

account name:parent namespace:owner:currency
e.g.
"kwh":"jw.gc.chep.mon.uk":"jw.chep.mon.uk":"kwh.gc.chep.mon.uk"


ALIASES

name:parent namespace:login identity
e.g.
"js":"global.cc":"jw.gc.chep.mon.uk"


LOGIN IDENTITIES

name:parent namespace:real name:email1:email2:password:PIN
e.g.
"fred":gc.chep.mon.uk":"":"fred@dodgy.com":"":"bAdpA55w0rd":"314159"

NB, optional fields (such as the user's real name or the second recovery email
address) can be left as empty strings, as in this example.




Such CSV files can be created easily using spreadsheet software.

The separators might be tabs or semicolons rather than semicolons.




Usage over SSH:

e.g. running on the user's command line (locally):


cat logins_026.csv | ssh -p 8804 slate_user_3@lrc.org.uk slate_import_logins

Where
   slate_user_3 	is the user's SSH login id
   8804 		is port number forwarded to VM running SLATE instance
   logins_026.csv 	is CSV file with the details to create a set of logins
   slate_import_logins  is the script that parses the CSV file to create these

This means that the CSV file does not need to be uploaded to the server
